findings.body:
The `From<tray_icon::MouseButtonState>` impl used to map `Up` to `Up` and `Down` to `Down`. This PR swaps them, so every tray click now reports the opposite button state. JS listeners that check `buttonState === "Down"` to detect a click will see the wrong value, and Rust listeners that match on `MouseButtonState::Up` will be wrong too.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="31-38"}

The existing `tray_event_json_serialization` test only checks serde output of a hand-built `TrayIconEvent`. It never exercises the `From` conversion, which is why this inversion passed. Add a unit test that converts each `tray_icon::MouseButtonState` variant and asserts the matching Tauri variant.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="31-38"}
impl From<tray_icon::MouseButtonState> for MouseButtonState {
  fn from(value: tray_icon::MouseButtonState) -> Self {
    match value {
      tray_icon::MouseButtonState::Up => MouseButtonState::Up,
      tray_icon::MouseButtonState::Down => MouseButtonState::Down,
    }
  }
}

This PR adds `pub fn new_click` on the public `TrayIconEvent` enum. Nothing in the repo calls it, and the PR description doesn't mention it. Once published, it's public API that needs maintenance and docs. The doc comment says it's for testing or mock events, which suggests it should be `pub(crate)` or behind `#[cfg(test)]`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="137-154"}

If the constructor is only needed by the test I'd suggest above, make it `#[cfg(test)] pub(crate)` or drop it. If it is wanted publicly, that should be a separate, documented API change.
